[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Unit Testing in Android: JUnit, Mockito, and PowerMock"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unit testing is an essential part of robust Android development. While JUnit and Mockito cover the majority of standard testing needs, Android developers often encounter legacy codebases or heavily coupled Android SDK classes (like static methods, final classes, or private methods) that require advanced mocking tools like PowerMock."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Setting Up Dependencies"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To get started with JUnit 4, Mockito, and PowerMock in your Android project (Java), configure your app-level build.gradle file with the appropriate testing dependencies."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"testImplementation 'junit:junit:4.13.2'
testImplementation 'org.mockito:mockito-core:3.12.4'
testImplementation 'org.powermock:powermock-module-junit4:2.0.9'
testImplementation 'org.powermock:powermock-api-mockito2:2.0.9'"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Using JUnit and Mockito for Standard Tests"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let us consider a standard Repository class that fetches user data from an ApiService. We can use JUnit for test execution and Mockito to mock the API dependency."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"public class UserRepository {
    private ApiService apiService;

    public UserRepository(ApiService apiService) {
        this.apiService = apiService;
    }

    public String getUserName(String userId) {
        return apiService.fetchUser(userId).getName();
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how you write the unit test using JUnit and Mockito annotations."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@RunWith(MockitoJUnitRunner.class)
public class UserRepositoryTest {

    @Mock
    ApiService apiService;

    @InjectMocks
    UserRepository userRepository;

    @Test
    public void testGetUserName() {
        User mockUser = new User("John Doe");
        when(apiService.fetchUser("123")).thenReturn(mockUser);

        String name = userRepository.getUserName("123");

        assertEquals("John Doe", name);
        verify(apiService).fetchUser("123");
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Overcoming Limitations with PowerMock"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need to test code that interacts with Android framework utilities or static methods (like android.util.Log or Math utilities) that Mockito cannot mock natively. PowerMock extends Mockito to handle static methods, final classes, and constructor instantiation."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public class UtilityClassUser {
    public int calculateScore(int base) {
        // Calls a static method from Android SDK or utility class
        return base * MathUtils.getRandomMultiplier();
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To mock static methods using PowerMock, you must use the PowerMockRunner and annotate your test class with @PrepareForTest."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"@RunWith(PowerMockRunner.class)
@PrepareForTest(MathUtils.class)
public class UtilityClassUserTest {

    @Test
    public void testCalculateScoreWithStaticMock() {
        PowerMockito.mockStatic(MathUtils.class);
        when(MathUtils.getRandomMultiplier()).thenReturn(2);

        UtilityClassUser utilityClassUser = new UtilityClassUser();
        int result = utilityClassUser.calculateScore(10);

        assertEquals(20, result);
        PowerMockito.verifyStatic(MathUtils.class);
        MathUtils.getRandomMultiplier();
    }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Prefer dependency injection and standard Mockito over PowerMock when possible, as PowerMock slows down test execution."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include @PrepareForTest when mocking static or final classes to avoid ClassNotFound exceptions."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep your Android unit tests running on the JVM (local unit tests in src/test/java) for fast feedback loops."}]
